PREBUILT BINARIES

Four files are built from other repositories or from third-party data and committed as binaries, because the
AOSP build does not build them. Each has a record with its source, toolchain, build command and SHA-256.

| File | Built from | Record |
| ---- | ---------- | ------ |
| airpods/core/airpods-core.jar | `airpods-core` in oblivios-services | airpods/SOURCE.md |
| passwords/core/arm64/libvault_core.so | `vault-core` in oblivios-services (Rust; its Cargo.lock lists 72 packages from crates.io) | passwords/core/SOURCE.md; licence texts of the 72 packages in passwords/core/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt; licences of the crates in oblivios-services `vault-core/SOURCE.md` |
| privacy/exit/daemon/arm64/exitd | `exit-daemon` in oblivios-services (Rust; its Cargo.lock lists 548 packages from crates.io and 4 in-tree packages) | privacy/exit/daemon/SOURCE.md; licence texts of the 548 packages in privacy/exit/daemon/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt; licences of the packages in oblivios-services `exit-daemon/NOTICE` |
| datamap/app/res/raw/land.bin | Natural Earth, see above | datamap/app/SOURCES.md |

Cargo.lock lists every package that any target or configuration may resolve; which of them a binary links
has not been computed. passwords/core/check-prebuilt.sh and privacy/exit/daemon/check-prebuilt.sh compare each
binary with its recorded hash. The two THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt files hold the licence files of the crates of each lock, made
by oblivios-services `exit-daemon/tools/third-party-licences.py` on 2026-11-17: they list every package of the
lock, so more than a binary links, and a package whose crate has no licence file is listed with the licence
and authors that its Cargo.toml declares. Whether that is enough notice for each of those licences is
not a legal opinion. The SBOM in oblivios-docs (sbom/) lists
the packages, their licences and the advisories that OSV names for them.

